1
00:00:31,950 --> 00:00:37,150
This is Walnut DataBridge, built with Tencent CodeBuddy and wired for Tencent's Hunyuan.

2
00:00:37,150 --> 00:00:39,550
Let's start with Aspire's own question.

3
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:44,200
Rachel is the money-laundering reporting officer at a fintech.

4
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:48,600
She asks Aspire's question: why has Velquarry Trading's account been restricted?

5
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:54,200
One answer, and every sentence points at a passage she can open right now.

6
00:00:54,200 --> 00:01:02,200
Click one, and you see the exact lines, the version, and when it was checked with the source: seconds ago.

7
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:07,400
Anything she cannot open at that moment is left out of the answer.

8
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:11,800
And now the screens you ask from work in Chinese too.

9
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:15,000
Developer pages stay in English, and say so.

10
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:25,450
Here is everything Jordan, a backend engineer, can open, as one graph, each item checked with its source.

11
00:01:25,450 --> 00:01:30,250
Company A is a synthetic company; every name here is made up.

12
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:37,850
Compare two people, and Ken, an outside contractor, has a small corner of the company, with no greyed-out boxes.

13
00:01:37,850 --> 00:01:40,650
For Ken, the rest simply isn't there.

14
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:49,500
Now the same question from two people: Amy, an account manager, left; Rachel, right.

15
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:52,300
And what should I tell the customer?

16
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:57,100
Amy gets the approved customer script, with its sources, and nothing else.

17
00:01:57,100 --> 00:02:01,500
Rachel gets the actual case: the flagged transfers, and the review.

18
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:03,500
Same system, two different truths.

19
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:09,100
In Singapore, telling Amy that a suspicious-transaction report even exists is a criminal offence.

20
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:11,100
So the system never does.

21
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:14,300
The question is word for word the same.

22
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:17,100
Only who is signed in is different.

23
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:21,900
Who you are comes from your signed sign-in, never from the question.

24
00:02:26,550 --> 00:02:28,550
Now Ken on his own.

25
00:02:28,550 --> 00:02:31,350
On the left, he asks about Velquarry.

26
00:02:31,350 --> 00:02:34,950
On the right, about a company that doesn't exist.

27
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:37,750
The answers are identical, word for word.

28
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:40,550
A restricted file looks exactly like nothing.

29
00:02:40,550 --> 00:02:44,150
No count, and no hint that anything is hidden.

30
00:02:44,150 --> 00:02:47,750
We ran four hundred pairs like this: zero differences.

31
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:52,150
So nobody can find out which customers exist just by asking.

32
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:57,800
And now Amy's answer can become the reply itself.

33
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:04,200
She asks for a draft: the approved wording, built from the cited facts, and never sent.

34
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:09,400
Jordan's answer offers a ticket, but it's only a proposal until he confirms.

35
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:12,000
He confirms, and the ticket exists, with a link.

36
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,200
If a draft strays from the facts, the cited sentences are used instead.

37
00:03:19,850 --> 00:03:27,850
Answers can also become files: a table whose figures point at their sources, and a chart from the same facts.

38
00:03:27,850 --> 00:03:31,050
Or a PowerPoint file, built without a model.

39
00:03:31,050 --> 00:03:35,850
The table is built straight from the cited facts, with no model.

40
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:48,300
And if you're new here, there's a guided tour, and you can dictate a question; nothing is sent until you press Ask.

41
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:53,500
The value calculator lets you change every input; the result is an estimate.

42
00:03:55,150 --> 00:03:57,150
And the brain keeps up.

43
00:03:57,150 --> 00:04:02,750
Jordan posts a note in Slack, and it is in his graph straight away.

44
00:04:02,750 --> 00:04:07,950
Ken's graph, right beside it, shows nothing new: he cannot open that channel.

45
00:04:07,950 --> 00:04:14,350
A moment later Jordan asks about the note, and the answer quotes it, with its source.

46
00:04:14,350 --> 00:04:16,150
Nothing stale.

47
00:04:16,150 --> 00:04:22,150
The post is checked with Slack and written to the audit log, like everything else.

48
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:30,000
New files can be added too, into a folder, and the upload is written to the audit log.

49
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,800
PDF, Office and text files are accepted.

50
00:04:37,450 --> 00:04:39,850
Now the compliance side, as Maya.

51
00:04:39,850 --> 00:04:46,650
The app has two modes: User, for asking, and Developer, for seeing how an answer was made.

52
00:04:46,650 --> 00:04:49,450
This is Rachel's request, end to end.

53
00:04:49,450 --> 00:04:55,850
Each bar is a real timing: two permission checks first, then the answer is put together.

54
00:04:55,850 --> 00:04:58,250
Those timings are measured, not drawn.

55
00:04:58,250 --> 00:05:03,050
These three records were sealed and chained before Rachel saw a word.

56
00:05:03,050 --> 00:05:08,250
And below, every document that was considered, with its verdict and the reason.

57
00:05:08,250 --> 00:05:10,050
Maya sees every decision.

58
00:05:10,050 --> 00:05:12,450
She never sees the answer itself.

59
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:24,300
Maya can also ask the audit log in plain English: everything Rachel accessed in the last seven days.

60
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:27,500
She sees the filter first, then the rows.

61
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:33,100
Every question anyone asks is written to this chain before the answer is released.

62
00:05:33,100 --> 00:05:36,300
Verify re-checks every link in the chain: intact.

63
00:05:36,300 --> 00:05:38,100
Now the fun part.

64
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:42,100
The tamper demo copies the log and attacks the copy.

65
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:48,500
It changes one old decision, from "forbidden" to "verified", and Verify points at the exact entry.

66
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:51,700
Deleting or rolling back records is caught too.

67
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:56,900
And if the log cannot be written, there is no answer at all.

68
00:05:56,900 --> 00:05:59,300
The real log was never touched.

69
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:02,900
That's what we mean by answers you can prove.

70
00:06:06,750 --> 00:06:13,150
Maya's analytics come straight from the verified log: how many questions, how many answered, how fast.

71
00:06:13,150 --> 00:06:17,150
Seeded history is labelled, and one click leaves it out.

72
00:06:17,150 --> 00:06:20,350
And how often the live check said no.

73
00:06:21,400 --> 00:06:25,800
The Trust centre lists the seven controls behind all of this.

74
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:33,400
Each one is enforced in code, mapped to Singapore's AI and data rules, and proved by a named test.

75
00:06:35,050 --> 00:06:39,450
Nobody wants another app, so it also lives inside Tencent WorkBuddy.

76
00:06:39,450 --> 00:06:46,250
Each connector carries its own signed token, so Jordan's connector gets Jordan's answer, and Ken's gets Ken's.

77
00:06:46,250 --> 00:06:48,250
Same gates, same audit log.

78
00:06:48,250 --> 00:06:51,050
The agent never decides who you are.

79
00:06:52,350 --> 00:06:55,550
Underneath it all is Walnut, the evidence engine.

80
00:06:55,550 --> 00:07:02,350
Here is Company A as Jordan sees it: every entity, every link, every value with a pointer.

81
00:07:02,350 --> 00:07:07,550
Drag the time slider, and the company's knowledge rewinds to any past date.

82
00:07:07,550 --> 00:07:13,150
And any value can be traced to the exact row in the exact file.

83
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:22,200
Two checks before any model, a grounding check after, the record written first.

84
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:26,600
Tencent Hy3 is wired in through TokenHub, with a safe fallback.

85
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:32,600
Until it is switched on, answers are quoted from the sources, through the same checks.

86
00:07:33,650 --> 00:07:36,850
We didn't just demo it, we attacked it.

87
00:07:36,850 --> 00:07:40,850
Seven thousand three hundred and seventy-four adversarial requests: zero leaks.

88
00:07:40,850 --> 00:07:43,250
Four hundred paired questions: zero differences.

89
00:07:43,250 --> 00:07:50,450
And one thousand two hundred and seventy-eight backend tests, plus one hundred and eighty-five browser tests, none failing.

90
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:55,100
What's it worth?

91
00:07:55,100 --> 00:08:03,900
For a six-hundred-person fintech, if each person saves ninety minutes a week, that's about one point three million US dollars a year.

92
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:06,300
It's an estimate, not a measurement.

93
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:10,700
And it pays for itself at about seven minutes a week.

94
00:08:13,950 --> 00:08:17,950
We built this with Tencent CodeBuddy, nine sessions in all.

95
00:08:17,950 --> 00:08:23,550
It built the WorkBuddy connectors, wrote tests, and its security review found real bugs.

96
00:08:23,550 --> 00:08:25,350
All in the repo.

97
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:32,000
The cover art was made in Miora, from a written brief.

98
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,400
The prompts are in the repo.

99
00:08:37,250 --> 00:08:40,850
Every company's AI will be asked to prove itself.

100
00:08:40,850 --> 00:08:42,650
Walnut DataBridge.

101
00:08:42,650 --> 00:08:44,450
Answers you can prove.

102
00:08:44,450 --> 00:08:48,050
We'd love one pilot team at one regulated company.

103
00:08:48,050 --> 00:08:49,550
Thanks for watching!
